          "diff_sha256": "7a6c15e32fcd700c9657bdf1b70a5462a5769f49541cc0a6113e744f145b5b16",
          "response": "The member is **paused with paid access**. Staff are describing access; the participation screen is describing participation. \u201cActive\u201d conflates two independent facts.\n\n[terms.md](terms.md) records that ambiguity. [DESIGN.md](DESIGN.md) already establishes separate ownership, and the code follows it.\n\nThe smallest vocabulary proposal is:\n\n| Concept | Meaning and proposed wording | Owner |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Member | An enrolled person; unchanged | Existing membership contract |\n| Participation | **Participating** or **Paused** | `participation.py`, using `participation` |\n| Paid access | **Has paid access** or **No paid access**, at a specified time | `access.py`, using `now < paid_through` |\n\nAvoid unqualified **active/inactive** in staff language and screen labels. For this case, display **\u201cParticipation: Paused\u201d** and, wherever access is relevant, **\u201cPaid access: Yes.\u201d** Internal `\"active\"` values and function names can remain unchanged.\n\nKeep the existing ownership boundary: participation determines whether someone participates; access determines whether their paid period covers the current time. Screens consume those answers without inventing a combined member status. Pausing must not alter paid-through dates, refunds, or cancellation semantics.\n\nI checked the existing functions with synthetic cases: paused members retain access before the paid-through boundary; participating members lack access at or after it. Those counterexamples show why neither fact can stand in for the other.\n\nNo files or behavior changed; these wording choices remain a proposal.",
